From: kkw537 <171009921+kkw537@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] cpuidle: apple: power a core down for idle periods from 2 ms

The driver's deep state ("CPU PD", deep WFI) has an exit latency of
10 us but asked for 10 ms of idle time before it is worth entering. The
governor therefore leaves a core in plain WFI, clock-gated but powered,
whenever the next timer or the idle time it predicts is under 10 ms: a
core that a 120 Hz display (8.3 ms) or a short audio period wakes never
powers down at all. On an M1 Pro, over three hours that included a long
compile, the first efficiency core spent more time in WFI (5377 s) than
powered down (3836 s), and more than half of its deep-state entries were
ended early by a wakeup the governor had not expected so soon.

Entering the state costs cpu_pm_enter() and cpu_pm_exit() and saving a
few registers; nothing is reset and the core resumes in line. So ask for
2 ms instead. Where the state really breaks even has not been measured,
and a value that is too low costs energy instead of saving it, so make
it a parameter that can be changed while running:

  /sys/module/cpuidle_apple/parameters/pd_residency_us

or cpuidle_apple.pd_residency_us= at boot. 10000 gives the old
behaviour back. The governors read the state's target residency each
time they choose a state, so a new value applies from the next idle
period on.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-apple.c
+++ b/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-apple.c
@@ -8,6 +8,8 @@
 #include <linux/init.h>
 #include <linux/cpuidle.h>
 #include <linux/cpu_pm.h>
+#include <linux/kstrtox.h>
+#include <linux/moduleparam.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
 #include <asm/cpuidle.h>
@@ -18,6 +20,22 @@
 	STATE_COUNT
 };
 
+/*
+ * The shortest idle period, in microseconds, for which a core is powered
+ * down instead of clock-gated. Deep WFI is left within microseconds, and
+ * entering it costs cpu_pm_enter() and cpu_pm_exit(), so it pays off long
+ * before the 10 ms this driver used to ask for. That kept a core that
+ * something wakes more than 100 times a second (a 120 Hz display, a short
+ * audio period) out of the state altogether. Where it breaks even has not
+ * been measured, so the value can be changed while running, in
+ * /sys/module/cpuidle_apple/parameters/pd_residency_us, or at boot with
+ * cpuidle_apple.pd_residency_us=. 10000 gives the old behaviour.
+ */
+#define PD_RESIDENCY_US_DEFAULT	2000
+#define PD_RESIDENCY_US_MAX	1000000
+
+static unsigned int pd_residency_us = PD_RESIDENCY_US_DEFAULT;
+
 asm(
 	".pushsection .cpuidle.text, \"ax\"\n"
 	".type apple_cpu_deep_wfi, @function\n"
@@ -111,7 +129,7 @@
 			.enter			= apple_enter_idle,
 			.enter_s2idle		= apple_enter_idle,
 			.exit_latency		= 10,
-			.target_residency	= 10000,
+			.target_residency	= PD_RESIDENCY_US_DEFAULT,
 			.power_usage            = 0,
 			.name			= "CPU PD",
 			.desc			= "CPU/cluster powered down",
@@ -122,6 +140,39 @@
 	.state_count = STATE_COUNT,
 };
 
+static int pd_residency_set(const char *val, const struct kernel_param *kp)
+{
+	struct cpuidle_state *s = &apple_idle_driver.states[STATE_PWRDOWN];
+	unsigned int us;
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = kstrtouint(val, 0, &us);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+	/* Longer than WFI's, so that the states stay in order. */
+	if (us < 2 || us > PD_RESIDENCY_US_MAX)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	pd_residency_us = us;
+	/*
+	 * The governors read these without a lock, each time they choose a
+	 * state: one more choice by the old value does no harm. Before the
+	 * driver is registered, registering it works out the nanoseconds.
+	 */
+	WRITE_ONCE(s->target_residency, us);
+	WRITE_ONCE(s->target_residency_ns, (s64)us * NSEC_PER_USEC);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static const struct kernel_param_ops pd_residency_ops = {
+	.set = pd_residency_set,
+	.get = param_get_uint,
+};
+module_param_cb(pd_residency_us, &pd_residency_ops, &pd_residency_us, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(pd_residency_us,
+		 "Shortest idle period (us) for which a core is powered down");
+
 static int apple_cpuidle_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
 	return cpuidle_register(&apple_idle_driver, NULL);
